"""Original NCD and mental-wellbeing drill; first option is correct."""
RAW = '''
A noncommunicable disease is generally|not passed directly from person to person like an infection|always caused by a mosquito bite|always diagnosed from appearance|identical to every infectious disease|NCDs are generally not transmitted directly between people as infections are. Malaria is an infection, whereas diabetes is not spread directly like an infection.
Which is an example of an NCD?|diabetes|malaria|measles|tuberculosis|Diabetes is a noncommunicable condition. A category is useful for study but is not an individual diagnosis.
Which is an NCD category?|cardiovascular diseases|malaria parasites|measles infection|tuberculosis infection|Cardiovascular diseases are a major NCD category. The heart and blood vessels form the cardiovascular system.
Chronic respiratory diseases|affect breathing and lung function|are identical to all infectious diseases|are diagnosed solely from clothing|never need clinical care|Chronic respiratory diseases affect breathing and lung function. Clinical assessment is needed when symptoms are concerning.
Cancer refers to|a group of diseases involving abnormal cell growth|a single infection with one identical cause|only a bone fracture|a sports injury by definition|Cancers are a group of diseases involving abnormal cell growth. Different cancers have different causes and treatments.
Diabetes involves|problems regulating blood glucose|only a change in hair colour|a disease always spread by casual contact|a mosquito species|Diabetes involves problems regulating blood glucose; types and management differ. Individual diagnosis and treatment require qualified assessment.
Cardiovascular diseases affect|the heart and blood vessels|only bones and tendons|only the digestive tract|only a sports venue|Cardiovascular diseases involve the heart and blood vessels. Care and prevention depend on the person and condition.
An NCD can be influenced by|genetic, physiological, environmental and behavioural factors|only one fixed cause in every person|only a team name|only a person's appearance|NCDs arise from interacting factors that vary by condition and person. Risk is shaped by multiple interacting influences.
Some infections|can increase the risk of particular cancers|make every cancer directly contagious|always prevent cancer|are the only cause of all NCDs|Some infectious agents increase the risk of certain cancers without making all cancers contagious. The infection and the resulting cancer risk are distinct concepts.
A risk factor|changes probability without guaranteeing an outcome|proves every exposed person will develop disease|is a complete diagnosis|rules out other influences|Risk describes probability, not certainty or a complete diagnosis. A risk factor does not establish an individual diagnosis.
A person without one particular risk factor|may still develop a condition for other reasons|is guaranteed never to develop any condition|cannot need assessment|has a complete diagnosis|Many factors can contribute to disease risk.
Tobacco exposure|is associated with increased risk of several NCDs|guarantees the same outcome for everyone|eliminates cardiovascular risk|replaces medical assessment|Tobacco exposure increases risk of several diseases, though outcomes vary.
Air pollution can affect|respiratory and cardiovascular health|only the name of a sport|only a timetable|no aspect of health|Air pollution can contribute to respiratory and cardiovascular disease.
Harmful alcohol use|can increase the risk of injury and disease|is guaranteed harmless|prevents all NCDs|is a medical diagnosis by itself|Alcohol use can increase risks of injury and several diseases.
An unhealthy dietary pattern and physical inactivity|can contribute to NCD risk|guarantee a particular diagnosis|are the only influences on health|replace all inherited and environmental factors|Dietary patterns and activity can contribute to risk alongside other factors.
A modifiable risk factor is one that|may be changed or reduced through appropriate action|must be changed in exactly the same way by everyone|is always inherited|proves disease has already occurred|Some factors can be modified, though opportunities differ between people.
Age and inherited characteristics|are not readily changed|are always changed by an exercise plan|are identical in all people|provide a complete diagnosis alone|Age and inherited characteristics are not readily modified and do not alone establish diagnosis.
Prevention choices can be affected by|safe spaces, resources and access to services|knowledge alone in every situation|only a match score|only the colour of a facility|Resources and environments influence whether preventive actions are feasible.
A health programme can support prevention by|combining information with access to suitable services|blaming people for all barriers|promising zero risk|ignoring environmental conditions|Education, access and supportive environments can work together.
Screening decisions should|follow relevant guidance and individual professional advice|be identical for everyone regardless of age or risk|replace all clinical assessment|be made solely from appearance|Screening depends on current guidance, age, risk and circumstances.
A person taking prescribed medicine should|consult a professional before making changes|stop solely because a textbook discusses exercise|share the prescription automatically|assume all treatments are identical|Do not change prescribed care without appropriate individual advice.
Mental health is|an integral part of health|unrelated to physical or social wellbeing|the same as a sports score|always visible from appearance|Mental health is part of overall health and interacts with other dimensions.
A difficult day|does not by itself establish a mental health diagnosis|always proves a disorder|means support is never useful|identifies the cause of all illness|A diagnosis requires appropriate assessment, not a single experience.
Persistent distress that affects daily life|can warrant qualified assessment and support|must always be ignored|is automatically a character flaw|can be diagnosed from a diagram|Ongoing distress or difficulty functioning may warrant professional assessment.
A mental health condition|is not a moral failing|proves weakness of character|justifies discrimination|can always be judged by appearance|Mental health conditions are health concerns, not moral failures.
A supportive response to someone in distress is to|listen respectfully and help them seek appropriate support|ridicule them|diagnose them immediately|promise a universal cure|Listening and helping the person access support can be useful.
Social connection and appropriate activity|can support wellbeing but do not replace needed treatment|guarantee a cure for every condition|make professional care unnecessary in every case|prove an individual diagnosis|Supportive practices can help wellbeing but may not substitute for treatment.
A person at immediate risk of self-harm|needs urgent local emergency or crisis support|should always be left alone|must be blamed for distress|can wait indefinitely without help|Immediate danger calls for urgent help; stay with the person when safe and involve appropriate services.
When safety is at immediate risk, a helper should|seek appropriate emergency or safeguarding support|promise secrecy regardless of danger|ignore the concern|make a diagnosis from appearance|Immediate safety can require involving emergency or safeguarding services.
Stigma about mental health can|discourage people from seeking support|guarantee that services are accessible|replace treatment|establish a diagnosis|Stigma can create barriers to support and care.
Sudden severe chest pain can|require urgent emergency assessment|always be diagnosed from a book|be ignored if the person is young|prove one diagnosis in every case|Severe chest pain can be an emergency and requires prompt assessment.
Sudden one-sided weakness and difficulty speaking|can indicate an emergency|always mean a person is tired|should wait several days|can be diagnosed from clothing|Sudden neurological symptoms can be signs of an emergency.
Severe breathing difficulty or fainting|can require urgent help|never requires assessment|is always caused by one condition|is a fitness test|Concerning symptoms can have serious causes and require timely help.
A study guide|cannot replace individual diagnosis or emergency care|can diagnose every condition|eliminates the need for healthcare|makes local emergency procedures irrelevant|Educational material is not a substitute for professional assessment.
A person's body size alone|does not provide a complete health diagnosis|reveals every condition|proves the cause of all symptoms|replaces screening guidance|Appearance and size alone do not establish a complete diagnosis.
Risk reduction is most accurately described as|lowering likelihood where possible, not guaranteeing prevention|removing all possibility of illness|proving every individual cause|eliminating the role of care|Risk reduction is probabilistic and cannot guarantee an outcome.
Respecting privacy|supports dignity while recognising safeguarding duties|means never seeking help in an immediate emergency|replaces medical consent|guarantees no health problems|Privacy matters, but immediate safety and safeguarding can require appropriate action.
Long-term condition management|should be tailored to the person with qualified guidance|is identical for every person|never involves follow-up|can always be determined from one symptom|Management depends on diagnosis, circumstances and professional assessment.
A community can help reduce NCD risk by|supporting access to safe activity, cleaner air and appropriate care|assuming all people have identical resources|eliminating health information|ignoring service barriers|Community conditions and access influence prevention and care.
Which comparison is accurate?|NCDs and infections differ in transmission, though some infections can increase cancer risk|All cancers are passed by hugging|Every NCD is caused by a mosquito|An infectious agent cannot influence any cancer risk|Some infections increase certain cancer risks without making all cancers contagious.
'''
ITEMS = []
for line in RAW.strip().splitlines():
    stem, correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3, explanation = line.split('|')
    ITEMS.append((stem, (correct, wrong1, wrong2, wrong3), explanation))
